<section id="cost-gap"><h2>The cost gap, stated plainly</h2>
  <p>A quality portable plus manual transfer inlet runs roughly <strong>$1,500–$4,000 installed</strong>; a permanent standby runs <strong>$9,000–$16,000+</strong>. The gap buys: automatic 10–20-second transfer, whole-home capacity, no refueling runs, no extension cords, and dramatically lower CO risk. Whether that is worth 3–4× the money depends on outage frequency, medical needs, travel, and WFH exposure — not on badge loyalty.</p></section>

  <section id="compare"><h2>Head-to-head</h2>
  <ComparisonTable caption="Standby versus portable" headers={['', 'Standby', 'Portable + inlet']} firstColSticky={true} rows={[
    ['All-in cost', '$9,000–$16,000+', '$1,500–$4,000'],
    ['Transfer', 'Automatic, 10–20 s', 'Manual, minutes + setup'],
    ['Capacity', '10–28 kW whole-home', '5–12 kW essentials'],
    ['Fuel', 'NG piped / large LP tank', 'Gasoline cans / small LP (refill runs)'],
    ['Runtime', 'Days–weeks unattended', 'Hours per tank; needs attendance'],
    ['Power quality', '<5% THD (safe electronics)', '8–25% THD (risks boards/TVs)'],
    ['Noise', '61–68 dB, fixed placement', '70–80+ dB, wherever you put it'],
    ['Maintenance', '$300–$600/yr serviced', 'Oil, stabilizer, exercise discipline'],
  ]} /></section>

  <section id="safety"><h2>Safety & CO reality</h2>
  <p>Portables cause ~75 US CO deaths yearly (CPSC) — garage placement, rain improvisation, and missing detectors. Standby's fixed placement, engineered exhaust, and automatic operation remove the exact decisions that kill. A portable run safely (fully outdoors 20+ ft, exhaust away, CO alarms, never backfed) is legitimate budget backup; a portable run conveniently is the statistic. Full protocol: <a href="/guides/generator-safety-carbon-monoxide/">CO safety guide</a>.</p>
  <ProConBox pros={['1/3 the cost; no permits/pad/gas work', 'Powers essentials fine; portable utility', 'No standby maintenance contract']} cons={['Manual everything, in the storm, at 2 AM', 'Refueling logistics; gasoline degrades', 'High THD + high CO risk profile']} prosTitle="Portable wins when…" consTitle="…but accept" />
  </section>

  <section id="decide"><h2>Decision framework (5 questions)</h2>
  <ul><li>Outages longer than a day most years? → standby.</li><li>Medical devices, sump, or well water? → standby (or portable + UPS + strict plan).</li><li>Travel frequently / elderly household? → standby (automation is the product).</li><li>Brief summer blips, tight budget, home during outages? → portable + inlet, done right.</li><li>WFH with sensitive gear? → standby's clean power, or portable strictly through inverter + UPS.</li></ul></section>

  <section id="faq"><span class="sr-only">FAQ anchor</span></section>
  <FAQSection items={[
    { q: 'Is a standby generator worth it over a portable?', a: 'For frequent, long, or medical-critical outages: yes — automation, capacity, and safety justify 3–4× the cost. For rare brief outages with an able-bodied homeowner present: a properly connected portable is the rational buy.' },
    { q: 'Can a portable run my whole house?', a: 'No — portables cover essentials (fridge, lights, blower, small pump/AC) via a proper inlet or interlock. Whole-home loads need standby capacity. Never backfeed through dryer outlets.' },
    { q: 'Which is safer?', a: 'Standby, by a wide margin: fixed placement and automatic operation eliminate the user errors behind ~75 yearly portable CO deaths. Portables demand rigorous placement and detector discipline.' },
  
    { q: 'Can a portable power my furnace in winter?', a: 'Yes via a proper inlet or interlock — furnace blowers draw modest watts. Size the blower plus fridge and lights, and never backfeed.' },
    { q: 'How much fuel does a portable burn in an outage?', a: 'Far less in absolute terms, but gasoline needs refills every 8–12 hours and degrades in storage — the logistics tax standby owners never pay.' },
    { q: 'Should renters buy standby or portable?', a: "Portable (with landlord permission for inlet work) — standby is a permanent improvement to someone else's asset." },
  ]} />
  <section id="conclusion"><h2>Conclusion</h2><p>Buy automation and safety margin (standby) or buy adequacy on a budget (portable + inlet) — but never buy a portable and run it like a standby. Next: <a href="/comparisons/transfer-switch-vs-interlock/">the manual-side switch decision</a>.</p></section>
</ArticleLayout>
